 Eights is a premium hand in heads-up play, so Raising builds the pot when I am ahead, and ensures that my opponents will respect my lead.\n\nFinal Action: **200**\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: Pocket Eights (8s 8d) is a very strong starting hand in Heads-Up Texas Hold'em, ranking well above the average hand strength. With only two hands remaining after this one and currently holding the lead, the goal is to play profitable hands aggressively to maintain the advantage without taking unnecessary risks like over-betting. Folding is far too passive, and checking (limping) allows the opponent to see a free flop with potential overcards that could beat the eights. Raising is the standard play to take the initiative, build value, and protect the hand.\n\nReason: Raising to 200 (the minimum raise) asserts dominance and denies the opponent free equity while keeping the pot manageable compared to the stack size. It forces the opponent to pay to see the flop or fold, capitalizing on the hand's pre-flop strength.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
